# ============================================================
# SealedSecret — ArgoCD repository credential (github.com/dev-technotaau/hire-adda)
# ============================================================
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   All 7 ArgoCD Applications clone this repo over HTTPS:
#     https://github.com/dev-technotaau/hire-adda.git
#   Today that works ONLY because the repo is public — the cluster has no
#   repository credential at all (verified: zero secrets labelled
#   argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type=repository or =repo-creds).
#
#   The moment the repo is made private, every Application fails to sync with
#   401 and GitOps silently stops reconciling. Running pods keep serving, so
#   the breakage is invisible until the next deploy quietly does nothing.
#
# ── WHY THE .template EXTENSION (DO NOT RENAME TO .yaml) ────
#   `hire-adda-security` syncs this directory with directory.recurse=true and
#   syncPolicy.automated (prune=true, selfHeal=true). It therefore applies
#   EVERY *.yaml under infra/k8s/security automatically, within minutes.
#
#   Two consequences:
#     1. A placeholder with REPLACE_ME values would be applied verbatim; the
#        sealed-secrets controller cannot decrypt that and the Application
#        goes Degraded. (Nearly shipped exactly this.)
#     2. Even a VALID sealed secret does not belong here: the Application's
#        destination namespace is `hire-adda`, while this Secret must land in
#        `argocd`. ArgoCD would flag the namespace mismatch rather than apply
#        it cleanly.
#
#   So this file stays `.template` permanently. It is documentation and a
#   generator recipe — never a synced manifest.
#
#   BOTH this recipe and the live sealed credential now live in
#   infra/k8s/bootstrap/, which NO ArgoCD Application syncs (verified against
#   all 7 app source paths). The live sealed copy is
#   `argocd-repo-credentials.sealed.yaml`; see the README in that directory
#   for the restore and rotation procedures.
#
# ── READ THIS BEFORE MAKING THE REPO PRIVATE ────────────────
#
# BOOTSTRAP ORDER MATTERS — THIS FILE CANNOT DELIVER ITSELF.
#   This directory (infra/k8s/security) is synced by the `hire-adda-security`
#   Application. So if the repo is already private, ArgoCD cannot read this
#   file to obtain the credential it needs in order to read the repo. That is
#   a deadlock.
#
#   Therefore the correct sequence is:
#     1. Apply this secret MANUALLY on the cluster  (command below)
#     2. Confirm all 7 apps still show Synced
#     3. ONLY THEN flip the repo to private
#     4. Re-confirm: kubectl get applications -n argocd
#
#   This file is kept in git for disaster recovery and reproducibility, NOT as
#   the delivery mechanism for the initial bootstrap.
#
#   Note also that `hire-adda-security` targets destination namespace
#   `hire-adda`, while this secret must live in `argocd`. That is a second,
#   independent reason to apply it out-of-band rather than relying on the app.
#
# ── WHICH ACCOUNT OWNS THE PAT ──────────────────────────────
#   Use a PAT owned by **dev-technotaau** (the repo OWNER), not by an
#   individual collaborator.
#
#   Verified account facts:
#     dev-technotaau    type=User, owns the repo
#     chanderbhanswami  type=User, collaborator (pull/push/triage, admin=false)
#
#   A collaborator's access can be revoked, and if ArgoCD authenticates as that
#   collaborator, GitOps breaks the day their access changes. The owner cannot
#   lose access to their own repo.
#
#   PAT type: fine-grained, "Only select repositories" → hire-adda,
#             Repository permissions → Contents: Read-only.
#   Nothing more is required — ArgoCD only reads.
#
#   The `username` field is a formality: GitHub validates the PAT and ignores
#   the username, but ArgoCD requires it to be non-empty. Keep it matching the
#   PAT owner so the credential is self-documenting.
#
# ── GENERATE / ROTATE ───────────────────────────────────────
#   Run this ON THE VPS (kubeseal must reach the controller). It prompts for
#   the PAT with `read -s`, so the token never lands in shell history, in argv,
#   or on screen. Verified working against kubeseal 0.27.3.
#
#     read -rsp 'PAT for dev-technotaau: ' PAT; echo
#     kubectl create secret generic argocd-repo-hire-adda \
#       --namespace=argocd \
#       --from-literal=type=git \
#       --from-literal=url=https://github.com/dev-technotaau/hire-adda.git \
#       --from-literal=username=dev-technotaau \
#       --from-literal=password="$PAT" \
#       --dry-run=client -o yaml \
#     | kubectl label --local -f - \
#         argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type=repository --dry-run=client -o yaml \
#     | sudo kubeseal \
#         --controller-name sealed-secrets \
#         --controller-namespace kube-system \
#         --format=yaml \
#     > argocd-repo-credentials.yaml
#     unset PAT
#
#   The `kubectl label --local` stage is what matters: kubeseal 0.27.3 copies
#   the source Secret's labels into spec.template.metadata.labels for you
#   (empirically verified), so labelling the INPUT is what makes the output
#   correct. ArgoCD ignores any secret lacking that label, and a missing label
#   is the single most common way this setup silently fails.
#
#   The command emits the COMPLETE file — encryptedData and template both.
#   Copy it over this file wholesale; do not hand-edit the encrypted values.
#
#   The sealed output is safe to commit and safe to share. The raw PAT is not.
#
# ── VERIFY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#     kubectl get applications -n argocd          # all 7 → Synced / Healthy
#     kubectl -n argocd logs deploy/argocd-repo-server --tail=50 | grep -i auth
# ============================================================
apiVersion: bitnami.com/v1alpha1
kind: SealedSecret
metadata:
  name: argocd-repo-hire-adda
  namespace: argocd
spec:
  encryptedData:
    # PLACEHOLDER — this file is NOT usable until regenerated.
    # Run the GENERATE command above on the VPS and replace this whole file
    # with its output. Do not hand-write these values: each is an RSA-encrypted
    # blob bound to THIS cluster's sealed-secrets key AND to the
    # namespace+name pair above, so it cannot be typed, guessed, or moved.
    #
    # `type`, `url` and `username` are not secrets, but SealedSecret encrypts
    # every key of the Secret, so all four are sealed together.
    type: REPLACE_ME
    url: REPLACE_ME
    username: REPLACE_ME
    password: REPLACE_ME
  template:
    metadata:
      name: argocd-repo-hire-adda
      namespace: argocd
      labels:
        # REQUIRED. ArgoCD discovers repository credentials solely by this
        # label; without it the secret is inert and syncs still fail with 401.
        argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type: repository
    type: Opaque
